{"key": "g03", "title": "Particles of the Qur'an 3: 'Did Not', 'Surely' and 'O'", "subtitle": "The jussive particles, Inna and her sisters, and the particles of calling", "trigger_at": "2026-11-17T13:00:00.000Z", "body": {"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "captionedImage", "content": [{"type": "image2", "__img": "cover-g03.png", "alt": "Particles of the Qur'an 3: 'Did Not', 'Surely' and 'O'"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Particles of the Qur'an, part 3 of 5", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"textAlign": null, "level": 3}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Five particles that make a verb jussive"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The jussive ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "jazm", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": ", جَزْم) is the mood where the verb ends in sukun: يَنْصُرُ \"he helps\" becomes يَنْصُرْ. If the verb ends in ن, as in تُبْدُونَ \"you show\", the ن drops: تُبْدُوا. These five particles cause it. The first four examples use the verb نَصَرَ \"to help\"."}]}, {"type": "ordered_list", "attrs": {"start": 1, "type": null, "order": 1}, "content": [{"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "if:", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " إِنْ ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "in", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": "), the particle of condition. إِن تُبْدُوا الصَّدَقَاتِ فَنِعِمَّا هِيَ \"If you give charity openly, that is good\" (2:271)."}]}]}, {"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "did not:", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " لَمْ ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "lam", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": "). It turns a present verb into a past denial: لَمْ يَنْصُرْ \"he did not help\"."}]}]}, {"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "not yet:", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " لَمَّا ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "lammaa", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": "). لَمَّا يَنْصُرْ \"he has not helped yet\" (but he still may)."}]}]}, {"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "let him:", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " the لِ of command ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "laam al-amr", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": ", لَامُ الْأَمْرِ). لِيَنْصُرْ \"let him help, he should help\"."}]}]}, {"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "do not:", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " the لَا of forbidding ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "laa an-nahy", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": ", لَا النَّهْيِ). لَا تَنْصُرْ \"do not help\"."}]}]}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"textAlign": null, "level": 3}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Inna and her sisters"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Six particles come before a nominal sentence. They make its subject accusative (fatha) and leave the predicate nominative (damma). They are called \"the particles resembling a verb\" ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "al-huroof al-mushabbahah bil-fi'l", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": ", الْحُرُوفُ الْمُشَبَّهَةُ بِالْفِعْلِ), or simply "}, {"type": "text", "text": "inna wa akhawaatuhaa", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " (إِنَّ وَأَخَوَاتُهَا, \"inna and her sisters\")."}]}, {"type": "ordered_list", "attrs": {"start": 1, "type": null, "order": 1}, "content": [{"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "surely:", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " إِنَّ ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "inna", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": "). إِنَّ اللَّهَ مَعَ الصَّابِرِينَ \"Surely Allah is with the patient\" (2:153). اللَّهَ takes fatha."}]}]}, {"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "that (surely):", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " أَنَّ ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "anna", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": "). أَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا رَسُولُ اللَّهِ \"I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah\" (from the call to prayer)."}]}]}, {"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "as if, like:", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " كَأَنَّ ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "ka-anna", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": "). كَأَنَّهُنَّ الْيَاقُوتُ وَالْمَرْجَانُ \"as if they were rubies and coral\" (55:58)."}]}]}, {"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "but:", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " لَٰكِنَّ ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "laakinna", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": "). وَلَٰكِنَّ أَكْثَرَ النَّاسِ لَا يَعْلَمُونَ \"but most people do not know\" (7:187)."}]}]}, {"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "if only, I wish:", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " لَيْتَ ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "layta", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": "). يَا لَيْتَنِي كُنتُ تُرَابًا \"Oh, I wish I were dust!\" (78:40)."}]}]}, {"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "perhaps, so that:", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " لَعَلَّ ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "la'alla", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": "). لَعَلَّكُمْ تُفْلِحُونَ \"so that you may succeed\" (2:189)."}]}]}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"textAlign": null, "level": 3}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The particles of calling"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The particles of calling ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "huroof an-nidaa'", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": ", حُرُوفُ النِّدَاءِ) are used to address someone: يَا \"O\", أَيَا \"O\", هَيَا \"O (someone far)\", أَيْ \"O (someone near)\", and أَ \"O (someone very near)\". By far the most common in the Qur'an is يَا \"O\"."}]}, {"type": "bullet_list", "content": [{"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Accusative:", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " when the one called is the first noun of a possessive pair, it takes fatha. يَا أَهْلَ الْكِتَابِ \"O People of the Book\" (3:64)."}]}]}, {"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Damma, fixed:", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " a single name takes damma, and it does not change. يَا نُوحُ \"O Noah\" (11:32)."}]}]}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"textAlign": null, "level": 3}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Two more"}]}, {"type": "bullet_list", "content": [{"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "No … at all:", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " لَا that denies a whole kind ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "laa an-naafiyah lil-jins", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": ", لَا النَّافِيَةُ لِلْجِنْسِ) makes its noun accusative. لَا رَيْبَ فِيهِ \"there is no doubt at all in it\" (2:2)."}]}]}, {"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Is not:", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " مَا and لَا with the meaning of لَيْسَ \"is not\" make the predicate accusative. مَا هَٰذَا بَشَرًا \"this is not a human being\" (12:31)."}]}]}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Next:", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " particles that change nothing at all."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "From my book", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " Quran Grammar Summary "}, {"type": "text", "text": "(second edition), section 1.5.", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Download this post as a PDF", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": ", with all the Arabic in Qur'anic script: "}, {"type": "text", "text": "Particles of the Qur'an 3: 'Did Not', 'Surely' and 'O' (PDF)", "marks": [{"type": "link", "attrs": {"href": "https://www.seemabooks.com/downloads/substack-posts/g03-did-not-surely-and-o.pdf", "target": "_blank", "rel": "noopener noreferrer nofollow", "class": null}}]}]}]}}